/*
 * The introduction and the guide pages of the API reference. The pages
 * describe the file format and the concepts of the library, which the
 * descriptions of the single functions refer to.
 */

/**
 * @mainpage libsdfio
 *
 * libsdfio is a portable, embedded-friendly and standard-conformant C library
 * without dependencies that reads and writes the SDF surface data files of
 * ISO 25178-71. An SDF file holds the heights of a profile or of an areal
 * surface measurement on a rectangular grid. It is used as a software
 * measurement standard, a set of reference data with which the software of
 * surface texture measuring instruments is verified, and as an interchange
 * format for topography data.
 *
 * @section features Features
 * - The revisions ISO-1.0 and ISO-2.0, each in the ASCII and in the binary
 *   format, and every data type that a revision allows: int8, int16,
 *   int32, binary32 and binary64.
 * - Two ways to the data. The coded values of the data area, as the file has
 *   them, and heights in metres as `float` or `double`. Buffers can lie in
 *   memory in any order, for example as a column of a structure, a channel of
 *   an image, or upside down.
 * - Files, blocks of memory and callbacks as sources and sinks. A reader needs
 *   no seek, so it also reads a pipe. It holds one buffer and never the whole
 *   file.
 * - Memory only when a handle or a file is created, through an allocator that
 *   the program can replace, so that a pool can be sized in advance. Reading
 *   and writing allocate nothing.
 * - Code for the formats that a program names. A program that writes binary
 *   files contains no code for ASCII or for reading.
 * - Handles without shared state, so that threads use their own handles at
 *   once. Standard C11, little-endian and big-endian processors, 32-bit and
 *   64-bit.
 * - Conformance to the standard. A writer writes what the standard defines; the
 *   text of a trailer is the program's own. A reader checks what the standard
 * defines and tolerates the deviations of files in the field, which are listed
 * in the description of sdfio.h.
 *
 * @section standard Standard and Heritage
 * The library implements ISO 25178-71:2026, "Geometrical product specifications
 * (GPS) -- Surface texture: Areal -- Part 71: Surface data file (SDF) file
 * format", in its versions ISO-1.0 and ISO-2.0.
 *
 * The format goes back to the BCR format that the European project on the
 * characterisation of roughness in three dimensions proposed in 1993. ISO-1.0
 * standardizes it, and some of the oddities of the format, such as the names
 * `NumPoints` and `NumProfiles`, come from that proposal. The library does not
 * support BCR-1.0 files. Its revisions are those of the standard.
 *
 * @section pages Guide
 * - @ref format describes the file: the three records, the header, the data
 *   area, the trailer and the two formats.
 * - @ref concepts describes the ideas of the library: coded values and heights,
 *   layouts, formats and configuration, streams, memory and errors.
 * - @ref states describes the life of a reader and of a writer: which call is
 *   valid when.
 * - @ref references lists the documents.
 * - The programs under Examples show typical uses. The interface is declared in
 *   `<sdfio/sdfio.h>`, which does not depend on the file functions of the C
 *   library, and in `<sdfio/file.h>`, which adds the functions for files.
 */

/**
 * @page format The SDF Format
 *
 * A file consists of three records: the header (record 1), the data area
 * (record 2) and the trailer (record 3). The file name extension is `.sdf`. A
 * file has an ASCII or a binary format. Both hold the same
 * information.
 *
 * @section format_versions Version Numbers
 * The file starts with a version number of 8 characters. Its first character is
 * `a` for the ASCII and `b` for the binary format, and the rest names
 * the version: `aISO-1.0`, `aISO-2.0`, `bISO-1.0` and `bISO-2.0`. The library
 * calls the version a revision, ISO-1.0 and ISO-2.0, because "version" names
 * the library.
 *
 * <table class="doxtable">
 * <tr><th>Revision</th><th>Differences</th></tr>
 * <tr><td>ISO-1.0</td><td>The data types int16, int32 and binary64. A binary
 *   file stores the numbers of columns and rows in 2 bytes each. The dates have
 *   no time zone.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>ISO-2.0</td><td>Adds the data types int8 and binary32. A binary file
 *   stores the numbers of columns and rows in 4 bytes each. The dates are UTC,
 *   and the trailer has the form of the header.</td></tr>
 * </table>
 *
 * Both revisions have a field for a compression and for a checksum of the
 * data. The standard defines only the value 0, for none, so a file has neither.
 *
 * @section format_header The Header
 * <table class="doxtable">
 * <tr><th>Field</th><th>ASCII Name</th><th>Binary
 *   Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 * <tr><td>Version number</td><td>none</td><td>8 characters</td><td>See
 *   above.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>Manufacturer's identifier</td><td><code>ManufacID</code></td><td>10
 *   characters</td><td>The source of the data, and possibly hardware and
 *   software.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>Creation date</td><td><code>CreateDate</code></td><td>12
 *   characters</td><td>When the data was generated.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>Modification date</td><td><code>ModDate</code></td><td>12
 *   characters</td><td>When the file was last modified.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>Columns M</td><td><code>NumPoints</code></td><td>2 or 4
 *   bytes</td><td>The points along x.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>Rows N</td><td><code>NumProfiles</code></td><td>2 or 4
 *   bytes</td><td>The profiles along y. 1 for a profile.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>X-scale</td><td><code>Xscale</code></td><td>binary64</td><td>The
 *   sampling interval along x in metres.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>Y-scale</td><td><code>Yscale</code></td><td>binary64</td><td>The
 *   sampling interval along y in metres, meaningless for a profile.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>Z-scale</td><td><code>Zscale</code></td><td>binary64</td><td>The
 *   positive factor that turns a coded value into metres.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>Z-resolution</td><td><code>Zresolution</code></td><td>binary64</td><td>The
 *   quantization step of the instrument in metres, or a negative number if it
 *   is unknown.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>Compression</td><td><code>Compression</code></td><td>1
 *   byte</td><td>0, no compression.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>Data type</td><td><code>DataType</code></td><td>1 byte</td><td>The
 *   code of the type of the values, below.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>Checksum type</td><td><code>CheckType</code></td><td>1
 *   byte</td><td>0, no checksum.</td></tr>
 * </table>
 *
 * The names `NumPoints` and `NumProfiles` come from the first versions of the
 * format, which were meant for tactile instruments that measure profiles. A
 * binary header has the fields in this order and has 81 bytes in ISO-1.0 and 85
 * bytes in ISO-2.0. In sdfio_header the identifier is a string, the dates are
 * a `time_t`, and the columns and rows are a `uint32_t`, the type of ISO-2.0.
 *
 * A date is a string of 12 digits, `DDMMYYYYhhmm`: day, month, year, hour and
 * minute, with leading zeros. The date of an ISO-2.0 file is UTC. The dates of
 * an ISO-1.0 file are the clock of the writer, whose time zone is not recorded,
 * and sdfio_time_zone converts them.
 *
 * @section format_data The Data Area
 * The data area holds the matrix W of the coded values with N rows and M
 * columns, row by row in the order of the position along y. The coded value
 * `w(i,j)` of the row i and the column j, counted from 1, belongs to the point
 *
 * @code
 * x = (j - 1) * Xscale     y = (i - 1) * Yscale     z = w(i,j) * Zscale
 * @endcode
 *
 * in a right-handed coordinate system: x grows with the column, y with the row,
 * and z points to the viewer who looks from above. The first row has y = 0.
 * Seen from above with x to the right, y grows upward, so the first row of the
 * file is the bottom row of the picture.
 *
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * seen from above:
 *
 *    y (along the rows)
 *    ^
 *    |
 *    |
 *    (o)------> x (along the columns)
 *
 *    z comes out of the page, to the viewer
 * @endcode
 *
 * <table class="doxtable">
 * <tr><th>Data
 *   Type</th><th>Code</th><th>Size</th><th>ISO-1.0</th><th>ISO-2.0</th><th>Enumerator</th><th>Value
 *   of a Point That Is Not Measured</th></tr>
 * <tr><td>binary32</td><td>3</td><td>4
 *   bytes</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td><td>SDFIO_TYPE_FLOAT</td><td>-3.402823466e38</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>int8</td><td>4</td><td>1
 *   byte</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td><td>SDFIO_TYPE_INT8</td><td>-128</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>int16</td><td>5</td><td>2
 *   bytes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>SDFIO_TYPE_INT16</td><td>-32768</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>int32</td><td>6</td><td>4
 *   bytes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>SDFIO_TYPE_INT32</td><td>-2147483648</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>binary64</td><td>7</td><td>8
 *   bytes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>SDFIO_TYPE_DOUBLE</td><td>-1.797693134862316e308</td></tr>
 * </table>
 *
 * A point that is not measured, or is spurious, has the lowest value of the
 * data type, the bad value. The lowest value is not permitted for a valid
 * point. An ASCII file writes the string `BAD` instead.
 *
 * @section format_trailer The Trailer
 * The trailer holds historical information about the measurement, such as the
 * name of the operator, the measurement conditions or the processing that was
 * applied. It is optional, has a variable length, and extends to the end of the
 * file. It is text in 7-bit ASCII. The first version of the format did not
 * define its content. An ISO-2.0 file uses the form of the header for it, a
 * line `Name = Value` for each field. sdfio_trailer_find_field() and
 * sdfio_trailer_next_field() find the fields in the text that
 * sdfio_read_trailer() returns.
 *
 * @section format_binary The Binary Format
 * The header has fixed fields in the order above, and nothing separates the
 * records. The header says how long the data area is, and what follows it is
 * the trailer. All numbers are stored with the least significant byte first, on
 * whatever processor the file is written or read. A string that is shorter than
 * its field is filled with spaces.
 *
 * @section format_ascii The ASCII Format
 * - The file consists of lines that end with a carriage return and a line feed.
 * - The first line is the version number. It is followed by a line `Name =
 *   value` for each field of the header, in any order, and the names do not
 *   depend on the case.
 * - The three records end with a line that consists of the character `*`, and
 *   the last such line ends the file.
 * - White space between the values of the data area is ignored, so the lines
 *   of the data area can have any length. The data area holds N times M values.
 * - A value of binary32 is written with 7 significant digits and a two-digit
 *   exponent, as in `1.500000e+00`. A value of binary64 is written with 15
 *   digits and a three-digit exponent, as in `1.50000000000000e+000`. The
 *   integers are written in decimal digits.
 *
 * @code
 * aISO-2.0
 * ManufacID = ISOTC213
 * CreateDate = 040120220853
 * ModDate = 050320231353
 * NumPoints = 3
 * NumProfiles = 2
 * Xscale = 1.00000000000000e-006
 * Yscale = 1.00000000000000e-006
 * Zscale = 1.00000000000000e-006
 * Zresolution = 1.00000000000000e-009
 * Compression = 0
 * DataType = 3
 * CheckType = 0
 * *
 * -1.000000e+00 6.948286e-01 3.170994e-01
 * 4.897644e-01 BAD 6.463130e-01
 * *
 * OperatorName = Jane Doe
 * *
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @page concepts Concepts
 *
 * @section concepts_values Coded Values and Heights
 * The data area stores coded values, one for each point, in the data type of
 * the file. A coded value is not a length. It becomes the height of the point
 * in metres when it is multiplied by the z-scale of the header.
 *
 * - A file of an integer type spends the range of the type on the heights of
 *   the measurement. The z-scale decides what one step of the integer is worth,
 *   so it trades the range against the resolution. The z-scale of a file that
 *   holds heights between -80 and +80 micrometres in int16 values is about
 *   2.4e-9 metres. sdfio_z_scale_for_range() and sdfio_suggest_z_scale() find
 *   the smallest z-scale that fits the heights.
 * - A file of a floating-point type usually holds metres directly, with the
 *   z-scale 1, but any z-scale is valid.
 *
 * The library offers both views. They are layers, and a program uses the
 * highest one that suffices:
 *
 * <table class="doxtable">
 * <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Functions</th><th>Use</th></tr>
 * <tr><td>Coded values</td><td>sdfio_read_data(),
 *   sdfio_write_data()</td><td>The values as the file has them, row by row. For
 *   a program that processes the integers of an instrument itself, or that
 *   transfers data without a conversion.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>Conversion</td><td>sdfio_decode_heights(),
 *   sdfio_encode_heights()</td><td>Converts rows between coded values and
 *   heights. Needs only a header, no handle.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>Matrices</td><td>sdfio_read_heights(),
 *   sdfio_write_heights()</td><td>The whole matrix as heights in metres, in one
 *   call.</td></tr>
 * </table>
 *
 * A function that encodes heights rounds an integer to the nearest value. A
 * height that does not fit the data type with the z-scale is an error
 * (SDFIO_ERR_RANGE), and so is a height that would be written as the bad value
 * (SDFIO_ERR_BAD_VALUE), because a reader would take it for a point that is not
 * measured. sdfio_write_heights() chooses the z-scale of an integer file if the
 * header has SDFIO_AUTO_Z_SCALE.
 *
 * @section concepts_coordinates Coordinate System
 * The points of a file lie in a right-handed coordinate system, as the standard
 * has it: x grows with the column, y with the row, and z, the height, points to
 * the viewer who looks from above. The point of the first row and the first
 * column is at x = 0 and y = 0, in metres. The row y = 0 comes first in the
 * file, and sdfio_read_data() and sdfio_read_heights() deliver the rows in that
 * order.
 *
 * The orientation matters when the matrix goes into a picture. Image libraries
 * count rows from the top, and the surface from y = 0 at the bottom, so a
 * program that shows a surface unchanged shows it upside down. A negative row
 * stride in the layout (see below) turns the matrix without a copy.
 *
 * @section concepts_missing Points That Are Not Measured
 * A point that is not measured has the bad value of the data type in the data
 * area. A height in metres has no such value, so the program picks its
 * representation: NaN, or a marker such as -9999 that
 * sdfio_layout::not_measured names. The conversion functions translate between
 * the two. A float marker is compared in the precision of the float, so -9999.1
 * marks the float nearest to it.
 *
 * @section concepts_layout Layouts
 * A buffer of the program is described by an sdfio_layout: the type of its
 * elements, the marker for a point that is not measured, and two strides. The
 * element at the row y and the column x is at the byte
 * `y * row_stride + x * element_stride` from the pointer that the program
 * passes. A plain array needs no strides (sdfio_layout_packed()). With strides
 * the matrix is read or written in place when it is part of something else:
 *
 * - the z member of an array of points, or a channel of an interleaved image;
 * - a matrix in column-major order, such as an Eigen matrix;
 * - a matrix upside down, with a negative row stride and the pointer at the
 *   last row, as many image libraries store it.
 *
 * The layout must keep the elements apart, which sdfio reports as an invalid
 * argument if it does not. Initialize a layout with sdfio_layout_init() or
 * sdfio_layout_packed() before assigning fields.
 *
 * @section concepts_handles Readers and Writers
 * A reader (sdfio_reader) reads one file, and a writer (sdfio_writer) writes
 * one. Both are opaque handles, created from a configuration, and freed with
 * their free function. A handle is not shared between threads, but different
 * handles can be used by different threads at the same time. The handles
 * hold no global state.
 *
 * @section concepts_sources Files, Memory, and Streams
 * A reader or a writer works on one of these:
 * - a file, with sdfio_open_file() and sdfio_create_file() of `<sdfio/file.h>`,
 *   which also has versions for wide-character paths on Windows;
 * - a block of memory, with sdfio_open_memory();
 * - a sdfio_stream, a pair of callbacks for reading or writing bytes, with
 *   sdfio_open_stream() and sdfio_create_stream(). A reader needs no seek, and
 *   a file is read once and in order. Rows can therefore be read only in the
 *   order of the file.
 *
 * @section concepts_modify Saving a Changed File
 * A program that opens a file, changes it and saves the result as a new file
 * copies the header of the file with sdfio_header_copy() and sets what
 * differs: the number of columns and rows of a part that it cuts out, the
 * modification date, or the format. The writer keeps its own copy of
 * the header when the file is created. The rows go from the reader to the
 * writer, and sdfio_copy_trailer() then copies the trailer, because the trailer
 * follows the data area. Fields of sdfio_write_trailer_field() can follow the
 * copy. A date that a reader reports as SDFIO_NO_TIME has to be set before the
 * header can be written. The example @ref excerpt.c shows these steps.
 *
 * @section concepts_options Options, Formats, and Memory
 * A handle is created with sdfio_reader_create() or sdfio_writer_create(),
 * which take only the allocator. sdfio_default_allocator() uses malloc() and
 * free(); a program that never names it does not contain them. The program then sets the options of the
 * handle, and the options are fixed by the first attempt to open or create a
 * file:
 * - the formats that the handle uses, with sdfio_reader_add_binary(),
 *   sdfio_reader_add_ascii() and the two functions of the writer. The
 *   library lists none by itself. A program that links the library statically
 *   contains the code of the formats it names and no other;
 * - the size of the buffer between the handle and its stream, and the number of
 *   values that are converted at a time, with the functions
 *   `set_buffer` and `set_chunk`. A handle that does not set them
 *   uses defaults;
 * - the time zone callbacks for the dates of ISO-1.0 files, with the functions
 *   `set_time_zone`.
 *
 * The options are functions, and a sdfio_header is opaque, so that the library
 * can gain options and header fields without a change of its structures.
 *
 * A header is a separate object. sdfio_header_create() creates one with
 * defaults and without dates, which a file needs: the setters or
 * sdfio_header_set_now(), the one function that reads the clock, set
 * them. The setters fill it, and sdfio_header_copy() copies one header
 * into another, for example the header of a file that was read into a header
 * for a file to write. sdfio_header_free() releases it. A handle allocates
 * memory only when it is created, and when a file is opened or created. The
 * handle comes first, and the buffer, the conversion chunk and what the format
 * needs come with the file. Reading and writing allocate nothing, and the
 * trailer is read into a buffer of the program.
 *
 * @section concepts_errors Errors
 * A function that can fail returns an sdfio_status, which sdfio_status_string()
 * names. The reader or writer also keeps a text that describes the most recent
 * failure (sdfio_reader_error_message(), sdfio_writer_error_message()). A
 * failure in the middle of the data area loses the position in the stream, so
 * the handle refuses further reads or writes afterwards.
 *
 * @section concepts_locale Locale
 * Numbers in ASCII files use the decimal point `.`, independent of
 * `LC_NUMERIC`. The program must not change the locale while a call is in
 * progress.
 */

/**
 * @page states Life of a Reader and of a Writer
 *
 * A reader and a writer move through states, and a call is valid only in some
 * of them. A call in another state returns SDFIO_ERR_STATE.
 *
 * @section states_reader Reader
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * sdfio_reader_create()
 *         |
 *         v
 *     [ new ]     the formats and the options are set in this state
 *         |
 *         | sdfio_open_file(), sdfio_open_memory(), sdfio_open_stream()
 *         | (the header is read and checked, the buffers are allocated)
 *         v
 *     [ open ]
 *         |
 *         | sdfio_read_data(), sdfio_read_heights()
 *         v
 *     [ reading rows ]
 *         |
 *         | all rows read, or sdfio_read_trailer() first
 *         v
 *     [ trailer ]
 *         |
 *         | an error in the data area or the trailer, at any point
 *         v
 *     [ failed ]  (every later read fails, the header stays available)
 * @endcode
 *
 * - A new reader reads nothing. The program lists its formats and may set its
 *   options. The first attempt to open a file fixes them, and the buffers are
 *   allocated then. A reader is opened once: opening it again is an error. If
 *   the open fails, sdfio_reader_error_message() says why.
 * - After the open, sdfio_reader_header() returns the header, which stays valid
 *   until the reader is freed.
 * - sdfio_read_data() reads rows in order, and sdfio_read_heights() reads the
 *   whole matrix. The latter works only before any row was read. A call that
 *   asks for more rows than remain is an invalid argument.
 * - When the last row is read, the end of the data area is checked. In an ASCII
 *   file a missing or an extra value is an error.
 * - sdfio_read_trailer() can be called at any time after the open. Rows that
 *   were not read are passed over. It returns the text in parts, and a part of
 * 0 bytes is the end. After the trailer was asked for, reading rows fails with
 *   SDFIO_ERR_STATE.
 * - An error in the data area or in the trailer ends the reading, and every
 *   later read fails with SDFIO_ERR_STATE. The header stays available.
 *
 * @section states_writer Writer
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * sdfio_writer_create()
 *         |
 *         v
 *     [ new ]     the formats and the options are set in this state
 *         |
 *         | sdfio_create_file(), sdfio_create_stream()
 *         | (the header is checked and not written yet, the buffers are
 *         |  allocated)
 *         v
 *     [ created ]
 *         |
 *         | sdfio_write_data(), sdfio_write_heights()
 *         | (the header goes out with the first row)
 *         v
 *     [ writing rows ]
 *         |
 *         | all rows written
 *         v
 *     [ trailer ]  sdfio_write_trailer_field(), sdfio_write_trailer_text()
 *         |
 *         | sdfio_end_trailer()
 *         v
 *     [ ended ]
 * @endcode
 *
 * - The program lists the formats of a new writer and may set its options. The
 *   first attempt to create a file fixes them, and the buffers are allocated
 *   then.
 * - The header is checked when the file is created. Nothing is written yet,
 *   because sdfio_write_heights() may still determine the z-scale from the
 *   heights. The header goes out with the first row, or with the end of the
 *   file if it has no row.
 * - sdfio_write_data() writes rows in order. sdfio_write_heights() writes the
 *   whole matrix, and works only before any row was written.
 * - The trailer can be written only when all rows are written, with
 *   sdfio_write_trailer_field() and sdfio_write_trailer_text(), in any order
 * and in as many calls as needed.
 * - sdfio_end_trailer() ends the file. It is required, also for a file without
 * a trailer, and nothing can be written after it. For a file created with
 *   sdfio_create_file() it flushes and closes the file.
 * - An error ends the writing, and every later write fails with
 *   SDFIO_ERR_STATE.
 *
 * Both handles are released with their free function in any state. A reader or
 * a writer for a file also closes the file then; a writer that was not ended
 * leaves an incomplete file.
 */

/**
 * @page references References
 * - ISO 25178-71:2026, Geometrical product specifications (GPS) -- Surface
 *   texture: Areal -- Part 71: Surface data file (SDF) file format.
 *   https://www.iso.org/standard/88787.html
 * - K. J. Stout et al., The Development of Methods for the Characterisation of
 *   Roughness in Three Dimensions, EUR 15178 EN, EC Brussels, 1993. The origin
 *   of the format (BCR-1.0).
 * - The Python package sdfio, https://github.com/aschet/sdfio, which reads and
 *   writes the same files, also in BCR-1.0, and produced the sample files of
 *   the tests.
 */
